---
# ===========================================================================
# ansible-lint — a ratchet, not a gate.
#
# What it is for here: stopping a NEW task from quietly breaking a convention
# this repository leans on — an unnamed task, a `command` whose `changed` is the
# module's guess instead of a statement, a file written with the wrong mode.
#
# What it is NOT: a correctness check. It cannot see inside a
# `lookup('template')`, so it does not catch the class of bug that actually costs
# a run in this repository — a wrong key name across an API boundary, or an
# interface named after its type. That class is covered by the manifest
# validator (filter_plugins/state.py + playbooks/tasks/validate_state.yml),
# which runs before either verb touches anything.
#
# It has to stay GREEN. A linter with 27 standing findings teaches you to ignore
# it, which is worse than not running it at all. So the rules that matter are
# enforced, and every deliberate deviation is written down here with its reason
# rather than silently suppressed.
#
# Run it from the repository root:  ansible-lint
# ===========================================================================
profile: basic

skip_list:
  # The manifest uses `>-` blocks and long Jinja one-liners deliberately: they
  # are read by people far more often than they are linted.
  - yaml[line-length]

  # configure/site.yml passes a role's variables explicitly at the call site
  # (`tw`, `caddy_sites`, ...) rather than letting the role reach into its own
  # defaults. That is the point of the call site: what a role is given is
  # visible where it is given. The rule wants every one of them prefixed with
  # the role's name.
  - var-naming[no-role-prefix]

  # This repository waits for a task by name (`when: X.changed`) instead of
  # notifying a handler, because the wait is part of the verification the run
  # prints. See the comment above the wait in playbooks/tasks/bridges.yml.
  - no-handler

  # Handler names here are lowercase and consistent across every role. The rule
  # wants Title Case; renaming them means editing nine `notify:` references
  # across four roles, and a `notify` that silently stops matching a handler is
  # exactly the failure this repository refuses to introduce for a capital
  # letter.
  - name[casing]

warn_list:
  # Deliberate `command`/`shell` where a module exists: these roles use the text
  # tools whose output they can read and assert on, and each one says why in a
  # comment above it. Warned rather than skipped, because the next one might not
  # have a reason.
  - command-instead-of-module

  # The rule is right in general and wrong for this code. Every one of the five
  # sites either runs with `set +e` on purpose (the read-only audit, which must
  # keep going after a failure) or is a read with `changed_when: false` whose
  # failure is caught by the assertion that consumes its output. Adding
  # `pipefail` mechanically would change behaviour, not fix it.
  - risky-shell-pipe
